I think that randomizing the dumplog entries just for those few that
want to cheat is hurting those that don't. Especially as it isn't a
thorough solution to the problem.

> I agree. And I think that this is in principle unavoidable; you can
> reduce the grade of abuse (by means like the ones you've implemented
> in the variants), but I think you cannot prevent leaking information.
> And we don't want restrictions WRT accessibility of dumplogs; they
> are used as reference in postings, they are used to do statistical
> evaluations, and they can be (at least partly) even educational in
> conjunction with the ttyrecs.
>
> IMO, not inspecting dumplogs should stay as a code of conduct in the
> responsibility of the players.

This is only an issue with vanilla as there is no development going
on. There are various options that could be done that make this
approach less effective.

NAO can only do so much as it wants to stay compatible. NAO already
has the bones option and with this the players can at least ensure
that they don't unwanted bones.

In UnNetHack when bones are generated it removes randomly some items
or if the inventory has more than 50 items removes items so there are
about 50 left. In the dumplog you also see how many bones levels were
encountered in the course of the game. The bones level loaded are not
always from the latest game of the bones ghost.

Of course this doesn't completely prevent information leakage from
bones. But I don't really care what the players do with bones and the
dumplogs as long as the information is recorded in the dumplog. After
all if they have fun playing the game this way and it doesn't bother
me, then I'll let them play.

